    <md:title>6.1.1: Understanding Polynomials</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4d730529-6ce3-4319-b975-0f14836740da</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>In this unit, remember to double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge, if needed.</para>
<para>A <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">monomial</term> is an algebraic expression with exactly one term: \(5x^2\)</para>
<para>A <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">binomial</term> is an algebraic expression with exactly two terms: \(5x^2 + 6y^3\)</para>
<para>A <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">trinomial</term> is an algebraic expression with exactly three terms: \(5x^2 + 6y^3 - 7c\)</para>
<para>Monomials, binomials, and trinomials are all different types of polynomials. A <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">polynomial</term> is a monomial or two or more monomials combined by addition or subtraction.</para>
<para>The <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">degree of a polynomial</term> is the highest degree of all its terms.</para>
<div class="os-raise-usermessage-link">
